{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lt9jsmenmUVFkGWJiRqMFn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd037-ln\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/ln: Create Links Between Files\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Build systems, package managers, and configuration tools create hard links and symbolic links\n6\t  to share files without duplication. The macOS BSD ln differs from GNU ln in backup semantics,\n7\t  --no-dereference behavior on symlink targets, and verbose output format. The Go implementation\n8\t  must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gln, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on\n9\t  all flag combinations and edge cases, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n10\t\n11\tgoals:\n12\t  - G1: Create hard links and symbolic links between files and directories.\n13\t  - G2: Support force-overwrite, interactive, and backup modes for safe link replacement.\n14\t  - G3: Support linking multiple targets into a directory in a single invocation.\n15\t\n16\trequirements:\n17\t  R1:\n18\t    title: Hard link creation (default)\n19\t    items:\n20\t      - R1.1:\n21\t          text: Must create a hard link from TARGET to LINK_NAME when invoked as 'ln TARGET LINK_NAME'.\n22\t      - R1.2:\n23\t          text: Must create hard links for multiple TARGETs in DIRECTORY when invoked as 'ln TARGET... DIRECTORY', placing each link in DIRECTORY with the same basename as the target.\n24\t      - R1.3:\n25\t          text: Must exit with a non-zero status and print an error to stderr when attempting to create a hard link to a directory.\n26\t      - R1.4:\n27\t          text: Must exit with a non-zero status and print an error to stderr when LINK_NAME already exists and neither -f nor -i is given.\n28\t\n29\t  R2:\n30\t    title: Symbolic link creation (-s, --symbolic)\n31\t    items:\n32\t      - R2.1:\n33\t          text: -s or --symbolic must create a symbolic link instead of a hard link.\n34\t      - R2.2:\n35\t          text: Must allow symbolic links to directories, unlike hard links.\n36\t      - R2.3:\n37\t          text: Must store the TARGET string as-is in the symlink, whether it is relative or absolute.\n38\t      - R2.4:\n39\t          text: -r or --relative must create a symbolic link with a relative path from the link location to the target, computing the relative path automatically.\n40\t\n41\t  R3:\n42\t    title: Force, safety, and verbosity flags\n43\t    items:\n44\t      - R3.1:\n45\t          text: -f or --force must remove existing destination files before creating the link.\n46\t      - R3.2:\n47\t          text: -n or --no-dereference must treat a destination that is a symlink to a directory as a regular file, rather than following it.\n48\t      - R3.3:\n49\t          text: |\n50\t            -i or --interactive must prompt the user on stderr before removing an existing\n51\t            destination. Prompt format: 'ln: replace 'DEST'? ' where DEST is the destination\n52\t            path. Read one line from stdin. If the response starts with 'y' or 'Y', proceed\n53\t            with removal and link creation. Any other response (including empty) means decline;\n54\t            do not remove the destination and do not create the link. Do not retry on invalid\n55\t            input. When stdin is not a terminal (detected via os.Stdin.Fd() isatty check),\n56\t            treat as decline (do not prompt, do not remove). When -f and -i are both given,\n57\t            the last flag on the command line wins, matching GNU ln behavior.\n58\t      - R3.4:\n59\t          text: -v or --verbose must print the name of each link created to stdout.\n60\t      - R3.5:\n61\t          text: -b must create a backup of each existing destination file before removal, using the default backup suffix '~'. --backup=METHOD must accept numbered, existing, simple, and none as backup methods.\n62\t      - R3.6:\n63\t          text: -S SUFFIX or --suffix=SUFFIX must use SUFFIX instead of '~' for backup file names.\n64\t\n65\t  R4:\n66\t    title: Differential testing\n67\t    items:\n68\t      - R4.1:\n69\t          text: Differential tests must compare stdout, stderr, exit codes, and resulting filesystem state between the Go binary and gln via pkg/testutils.\n70\t      - R4.2:\n71\t          text: \"Tests must cover: hard link creation, symbolic link creation (-s), force overwrite (-f), no-dereference (-n), verbose output (-v), backup creation (-b), multiple targets into a directory, relative symlinks (-r), and error cases for hard linking directories and existing destinations.\"\n72\t      - R4.3:\n73\t          text: Tests must verify that created links point to the correct target and have the correct link type (hard or symbolic), matching gln behavior.\n74\t\n75\tnon_goals:\n76\t  - cmd/ln does not implement -L or -P (logical/physical) dereference modes for following symlink chains on the target.\n77\t  - cmd/ln does not implement -t DIRECTORY or -T (--no-target-directory) flags.\n78\t  - cmd/ln does not read target names from stdin; all input comes from command-line arguments.\n79\t\n80\tacceptance_criteria:\n81\t  - id: AC1\n82\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/ln file1 file2 creates a hard link, and both files share the same inode, matching gln.\"\n83\t    traces:\n84\t      - R1.1\n85\t      - R1.2\n86\t      - R1.3\n87\t      - R1.4\n88\t  - id: AC2\n89\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/ln -s target linkname creates a symbolic link pointing to target, matching gln -s.\"\n90\t    traces:\n91\t      - R2.1\n92\t      - R4.2\n93\t  - id: AC3\n94\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/ln -sf newtarget existing_link replaces the existing link, matching gln -sf.\"\n95\t    traces:\n96\t      - R1.1\n97\t      - R1.2\n98\t      - R1.3\n99\t      - R1.4\n100\t  - id: AC4\n101\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/ln -s -r /usr/bin/sort ./sort_link creates a relative symlink, matching gln -s -r.\"\n102\t    traces:\n103\t      - R2.1\n104\t      - R2.4\n105\t      - R4.2\n106\t  - id: AC5\n107\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gln passes for hard links, symlinks, -f, -n, -v, -b, multiple targets, -r, and error cases.\"\n108\t    traces:\n109\t      - R1.3\n110\t      - R1.4\n111\t      - R2.4\n112\t      - R3.1\n113\t      - R3.2\n114\t      - R3.3\n115\t      - R3.4\n116\t      - R3.5\n117\t      - R4.1\n118\t      - R4.2\n119\t  - id: AC6\n120\t    criterion: \"cmd/ln compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n121\t    traces:\n122\t      - R1.1\n123\t      - R1.2\n124\t      - R1.3\n125\t      - R1.4\n126\t      - R2.1\n127\t      - R2.2\n128\t      - R2.3\n129\t      - R2.4\n130\t      - R3.1\n131\t      - R3.2\n132\t      - R3.3\n133\t      - R3.4\n134\t      - R3.5\n135\t      - R3.6\n136\t      - R4.1\n137\t      - R4.2\n138\t      - R4.3\n139\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d0c25e4-6f69-4e0e-8989-487121e9d072","uuid":"876769af-2dc7-412b-9e25-47c43f5d0e43","timestamp":"2026-05-20T11:59:59.395Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5344/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd037-ln.yaml","content":"id: srd037-ln\ntitle: \"cmd/ln: Create Links Between Files\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Build systems, package managers, and configuration tools create hard links and symbolic links\n  to share files without duplication. The macOS BSD ln differs from GNU ln in backup semantics,\n  --no-dereference behavior on symlink targets, and verbose output format. The Go implementation\n  must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gln, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on\n  all flag combinations and edge cases, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Create hard links and symbolic links between files and directories.\n  - G2: Support force-overwrite, interactive, and backup modes for safe link replacement.\n  - G3: Support linking multiple targets into a directory in a single invocation.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Hard link creation (default)\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: Must create a hard link from TARGET to LINK_NAME when invoked as 'ln TARGET LINK_NAME'.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: Must create hard links for multiple TARGETs in DIRECTORY when invoked as 'ln TARGET... DIRECTORY', placing each link in DIRECTORY with the same basename as the target.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: Must exit with a non-zero status and print an error to stderr when attempting to create a hard link to a directory.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Must exit with a non-zero status and print an error to stderr when LINK_NAME already exists and neither -f nor -i is given.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Symbolic link creation (-s, --symbolic)\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: -s or --symbolic must create a symbolic link instead of a hard link.\n      - R2.2:\n          text: Must allow symbolic links to directories, unlike hard links.\n      - R2.3:\n          text: Must store the TARGET string as-is in the symlink, whether it is relative or absolute.\n      - R2.4:\n          text: -r or --relative must create a symbolic link with a relative path from the link location to the target, computing the relative path automatically.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Force, safety, and verbosity flags\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: -f or --force must remove existing destination files before creating the link.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: -n or --no-dereference must treat a destination that is a symlink to a directory as a regular file, rather than following it.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: |\n            -i or --interactive must prompt the user on stderr before removing an existing\n            destination. Prompt format: 'ln: replace 'DEST'? ' where DEST is the destination\n            path. Read one line from stdin. If the response starts with 'y' or 'Y', proceed\n            with removal and link creation. Any other response (including empty) means decline;\n            do not remove the destination and do not create the link. Do not retry on invalid\n            input. When stdin is not a terminal (detected via os.Stdin.Fd() isatty check),\n            treat as decline (do not prompt, do not remove). When -f and -i are both given,\n            the last flag on the command line wins, matching GNU ln behavior.\n      - R3.4:\n          text: -v or --verbose must print the name of each link created to stdout.\n      - R3.5:\n          text: -b must create a backup of each existing destination file before removal, using the default backup suffix '~'. --backup=METHOD must accept numbered, existing, simple, and none as backup methods.\n      - R3.6:\n          text: -S SUFFIX or --suffix=SUFFIX must use SUFFIX instead of '~' for backup file names.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Differential tests must compare stdout, stderr, exit codes, and resulting filesystem state between the Go binary and gln via pkg/testutils.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: \"Tests must cover: hard link creation, symbolic link creation (-s), force overwrite (-f), no-dereference (-n), verbose output (-v), backup creation (-b), multiple targets into a directory, relative symlinks (-r), and error cases for hard linking directories and existing destinations.\"\n      - R4.3:\n          text: Tests must verify that created links point to the correct target and have the correct link type (hard or symbolic), matching gln behavior.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/ln does not implement -L or -P (logical/physical) dereference modes for following symlink chains on the target.\n  - cmd/ln does not implement -t DIRECTORY or -T (--no-target-directory) flags.\n  - cmd/ln does not read target names from stdin; all input comes from command-line arguments.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/ln file1 file2 creates a hard link, and both files share the same inode, matching gln.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/ln -s target linkname creates a symbolic link pointing to target, matching gln -s.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R4.2\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/ln -sf newtarget existing_link replaces the existing link, matching gln -sf.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/ln -s -r /usr/bin/sort ./sort_link creates a relative symlink, matching gln -s -r.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.4\n      - R4.2\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"Differential test against gln passes for hard links, symlinks, -f, -n, -v, -b, multiple targets, -r, and error cases.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.4\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R3.5\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"cmd/ln compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R3.5\n      - R3.6\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n","numLines":139,"startLine":1,"totalLines":139}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01M4oF7bRoP13N4pdP5sYKmd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R2St76qAnANXU7dUHfYUzY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5344/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd036-mktemp.yaml"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3008,"cache_read_input_tokens":23491,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3008},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d0c25e4-6f69-4e0e-8989-487121e9d072","uuid":"d4ae7d62-45b6-45bc-a1cd-05b25d6dca5d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R2St76qAnANXU7dUHfYUzY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd036-mktemp\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/mktemp: Create Temporary Files or Directories\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Shell scripts need unique temporary files and directories for safe intermediate storage.\n6\t  The macOS BSD mktemp uses a different flag set and template convention than GNU mktemp.\n7\t  The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gmktemp, installed by\n8\t  coreutils) on flag behavior, template expansion, and exit codes, verified by differential\n9\t  testing via pkg/testutils. Exact output comparison is not possible because generated names\n10\t  are random; tests must verify structural properties instead.\n11\t\n12\tgoals:\n13\t  - G1: Create a uniquely-named temporary file or directory and print its path to stdout.\n14\t  - G2: Support template customization for the generated name, including suffix and directory placement.\n15\t  - G3: Match GNU mktemp semantics for TMPDIR, -p, --tmpdir, and -t flag interactions.\n16\t\n17\trequirements:\n18\t  R1:\n19\t    title: Default behavior\n20\t    items:\n21\t      - R1.1:\n22\t          text: Must create a temporary file in the directory specified by the TMPDIR environment variable, or /tmp if TMPDIR is unset, and print the absolute path to stdout.\n23\t      - R1.2:\n24\t          text: Must use a template of 'tmp.XXXXXXXXXX' when no template argument is provided, where each X is replaced by a random alphanumeric character.\n25\t      - R1.3:\n26\t          text: When a TEMPLATE argument is provided, must replace the trailing sequence of X characters (minimum three) with random characters and create the file with that name.\n27\t      - R1.4:\n28\t          text: Must create the file with permission mode 0600 (owner read-write only).\n29\t      - R1.5:\n30\t          text: Must exit 0 on success and exit 1 on failure, printing an error to stderr on failure.\n31\t\n32\t  R2:\n33\t    title: Directory mode (-d, --directory)\n34\t    items:\n35\t      - R2.1:\n36\t          text: -d or --directory must create a temporary directory instead of a file.\n37\t      - R2.2:\n38\t          text: Must create the directory with permission mode 0700 (owner read-write-execute only).\n39\t      - R2.3:\n40\t          text: Must print the absolute path of the created directory to stdout.\n41\t\n42\t  R3:\n43\t    title: Template and location control\n44\t    items:\n45\t      - R3.1:\n46\t          text: -p DIR or --tmpdir=DIR must use DIR as the parent directory for the created file or directory, overriding TMPDIR.\n47\t      - R3.2:\n48\t          text: --tmpdir without a value must use TMPDIR or /tmp as the parent directory (same as default behavior), and interpret the next argument as a template.\n49\t      - R3.3:\n50\t          text: --suffix=SUFF must append SUFF after the random characters in the generated name. The template must not contain a slash after the X sequence when --suffix is used.\n51\t      - R3.4:\n52\t          text: -t must treat the template as a filename prefix in the TMPDIR directory, equivalent to legacy BSD compatibility mode in GNU mktemp.\n53\t      - R3.5:\n54\t          text: -u or --dry-run must print the name that would be created without actually creating it, and must print a warning that the option is discouraged.\n55\t      - R3.6:\n56\t          text: -q or --quiet must suppress error messages on failure.\n57\t\n58\t  R4:\n59\t    title: Differential testing\n60\t    items:\n61\t      - R4.1:\n62\t          text: Differential tests must compare exit codes and structural properties of output between the Go binary and gmktemp via pkg/testutils.\n63\t      - R4.2:\n64\t          text: \"Tests must verify structural properties: output is a valid path in the expected directory, the file or directory exists after creation, the name matches the template pattern, and permission bits match the specification.\"\n65\t      - R4.3:\n66\t          text: \"Tests must cover: default file creation, -d directory creation, custom template, --suffix, -p with explicit directory, -t legacy mode, -u dry-run, and error cases for invalid templates.\"\n67\t      - R4.4:\n68\t          text: Tests must not compare exact filenames between the Go binary and gmktemp, since random components will differ between invocations.\n69\t\n70\tnon_goals:\n71\t  - cmd/mktemp does not manage the lifecycle of created files; deletion is the caller's responsibility.\n72\t  - cmd/mktemp does not support creating multiple temporary files in a single invocation.\n73\t  - cmd/mktemp does not read templates from stdin; all input comes from command-line arguments and environment variables.\n74\t\n75\tacceptance_criteria:\n76\t  - id: AC1\n77\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/mktemp creates a file in /tmp matching the pattern tmp.XXXXXXXXXX and prints its path, structurally matching gmktemp output.\"\n78\t    traces:\n79\t      - R1.1\n80\t      - R1.2\n81\t      - R1.3\n82\t      - R1.4\n83\t      - R1.5\n84\t  - id: AC2\n85\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/mktemp -d creates a directory in /tmp with mode 0700, structurally matching gmktemp -d.\"\n86\t    traces:\n87\t      - R2.1\n88\t      - R4.3\n89\t  - id: AC3\n90\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/mktemp -p /var/tmp myapp.XXXX creates a file in /var/tmp matching myapp.XXXX, structurally matching gmktemp -p /var/tmp myapp.XXXX.\"\n91\t    traces:\n92\t      - R3.1\n93\t      - R4.3\n94\t  - id: AC4\n95\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/mktemp --suffix=.txt creates a file ending in .txt, structurally matching gmktemp --suffix=.txt.\"\n96\t    traces:\n97\t      - R3.3\n98\t      - R4.3\n99\t  - id: AC5\n100\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gmktemp passes for structural validation of default, -d, -p, --suffix, -t, and error cases.\"\n101\t    traces:\n102\t      - R1.5\n103\t      - R2.1\n104\t      - R3.1\n105\t      - R3.3\n106\t      - R3.4\n107\t      - R3.6\n108\t      - R4.1\n109\t      - R4.3\n110\t      - R4.4\n111\t  - id: AC6\n112\t    criterion: \"cmd/mktemp compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n113\t    traces:\n114\t      - R1.1\n115\t      - R1.2\n116\t      - R1.3\n117\t      - R1.4\n118\t      - R1.5\n119\t      - R2.1\n120\t      - R2.2\n121\t      - R2.3\n122\t      - R3.1\n123\t      - R3.2\n124\t      - R3.3\n125\t      - R3.4\n126\t      - R3.5\n127\t      - R3.6\n128\t      - R4.1\n129\t      - R4.2\n130\t      - R4.3\n131\t      - R4.4\n132\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6d0c25e4-6f69-4e0e-8989-487121e9d072","uuid":"2aa343bf-0940-4796-aeaa-70fcde11fc55","timestamp":"2026-05-20T11:59:59.855Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5344/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd036-mktemp.yaml","content":"id: srd036-mktemp\ntitle: \"cmd/mktemp: Create Temporary Files or Directories\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Shell scripts need unique temporary files and directories for safe intermediate storage.\n  The macOS BSD mktemp uses a different flag set and template convention than GNU mktemp.\n  The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gmktemp, installed by\n  coreutils) on flag behavior, template expansion, and exit codes, verified by differential\n  testing via pkg/testutils. Exact output comparison is not possible because generated names\n  are random; tests must verify structural properties instead.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Create a uniquely-named temporary file or directory and print its path to stdout.\n  - G2: Support template customization for the generated name, including suffix and directory placement.\n  - G3: Match GNU mktemp semantics for TMPDIR, -p, --tmpdir, and -t flag interactions.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default behavior\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: Must create a temporary file in the directory specified by the TMPDIR environment variable, or /tmp if TMPDIR is unset, and print the absolute path to stdout.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: Must use a template of 'tmp.XXXXXXXXXX' when no template argument is provided, where each X is replaced by a random alphanumeric character.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: When a TEMPLATE argument is provided, must replace the trailing sequence of X characters (minimum three) with random characters and create the file with that name.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Must create the file with permission mode 0600 (owner read-write only).\n      - R1.5:\n          text: Must exit 0 on success and exit 1 on failure, printing an error to stderr on failure.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Directory mode (-d, --directory)\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: -d or --directory must create a temporary directory instead of a file.\n      - R2.2:\n          text: Must create the directory with permission mode 0700 (owner read-write-execute only).\n      - R2.3:\n          text: Must print the absolute path of the created directory to stdout.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Template and location control\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: -p DIR or --tmpdir=DIR must use DIR as the parent directory for the created file or directory, overriding TMPDIR.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: --tmpdir without a value must use TMPDIR or /tmp as the parent directory (same as default behavior), and interpret the next argument as a template.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: --suffix=SUFF must append SUFF after the random characters in the generated name. The template must not contain a slash after the X sequence when --suffix is used.\n      - R3.4:\n          text: -t must treat the template as a filename prefix in the TMPDIR directory, equivalent to legacy BSD compatibility mode in GNU mktemp.\n      - R3.5:\n          text: -u or --dry-run must print the name that would be created without actually creating it, and must print a warning that the option is discouraged.\n      - R3.6:\n          text: -q or --quiet must suppress error messages on failure.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Differential tests must compare exit codes and structural properties of output between the Go binary and gmktemp via pkg/testutils.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: \"Tests must verify structural properties: output is a valid path in the expected directory, the file or directory exists after creation, the name matches the template pattern, and permission bits match the specification.\"\n      - R4.3:\n          text: \"Tests must cover: default file creation, -d directory creation, custom template, --suffix, -p with explicit directory, -t legacy mode, -u dry-run, and error cases for invalid templates.\"\n      - R4.4:\n          text: Tests must not compare exact filenames between the Go binary and gmktemp, since random components will differ between invocations.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/mktemp does not manage the lifecycle of created files; deletion is the caller's responsibility.\n  - cmd/mktemp does not support creating multiple temporary files in a single invocation.\n  - cmd/mktemp does not read templates from stdin; all input comes from command-line arguments and environment variables.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/mktemp creates a file in /tmp matching the pattern tmp.XXXXXXXXXX and prints its path, structurally matching gmktemp output.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R1.5\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/mktemp -d creates a directory in /tmp with mode 0700, structurally matching gmktemp -d.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R4.3\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/mktemp -p /var/tmp myapp.XXXX creates a file in /var/tmp matching myapp.XXXX, structurally matching gmktemp -p /var/tmp myapp.XXXX.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.1\n      - R4.3\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/mktemp --suffix=.txt creates a file ending in .txt, structurally matching gmktemp --suffix=.txt.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.3\n      - R4.3\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"Differential test against gmktemp passes for structural validation of default, -d, -p, --suffix, -t, and error cases.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.5\n      - R2.1\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R3.6\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.3\n      - R4.4\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"cmd/mktemp compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R1.5\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R3.5\n      - R3.6\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n      - R4.4\n","numLines":132,"startLine":1,"totalLines":132}}}
