orking directories, the TempDir() paths diverge between test runs, making direct comparison impossible.\n\nMy better approach is to skip RunDiffTests for success cases and instead run each binary separately, compare their exit codes, and validate the structural properties of each output independently. For error cases though, I can still use RunDiffTests since the stderr content should match after normalizing the binary name.\n\nWriting the test file... Writing test cases... Writing test cases...\n\nI'm realizing I need to handle temp file cleanup more carefully—the tests are creating actual files in /tmp that won't be automatically removed. I should defer cleanup calls and use test temp directories instead, maybe by setting TMPDIR environment variables to isolate each test's temporary files.\n\nLooking at the DiffTest struct, it has an Env field I can use to set TMPDIR for each test run. For the success cases, I could leverage RunDiffTests with a normalizer that strips out the random filename parts so both binary outputs match after normalization, since they'd be running in the same temp directory.\n\nSetting up test cases... I see how the test framework operates now—both binaries run in the same temporary directory with identical environment variables, so they generate output with the same paths. The normalizer strips out the random 10-character suffix from temporary filenames, converting paths like `/path/to/tmpdir/tmp.aB3dXHfoo` and `/path/to/tmpdir/tmp.kL9mNoPqR` into the same normalized form `/path/to/tmpdir/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX`, which allows the outputs to match after comparison.\n\nWriting the normalizer code... Writing the template validation logic... I'm checking what error messages gmktemp actually produces for different failure cases so I can match them exactly in my implementation. Looking at the template validation, collision handling, and argument parsing to make sure the output format is consistent with the real gmktemp behavior. Fixing the error message to use the original template argument instead of just the basename, so it shows the full path the user provided.\n\nNow I'm thinking about the \"too many templates\" error message—GNU mktemp shows that error followed by a help hint, but I need to be careful because not all errors should include that hint. The \"too few X's\" error doesn't have it, so I'll need to decide which errors warrant the help suggestion. Finishing the error handling structure...\n\nI'm realizing that by handling parse errors in main() with the \"Try\" message, I can keep runtime errors separate—they'll just print the error without the help suggestion. This matches GNU mktemp's behavior where argument parsing failures get the hint but template validation errors don't.\n\nFor the output path, when creating a file in the current directory without an explicit path, mktemp returns just the relative filename, not an absolute path, so I need to make sure that's what gets printed.\n\nBut there's a subtlety with relative paths like `./fooXXXXXX` — mktemp preserves that `./` prefix in its output, so I should too rather than letting filepath.Join normalize it away. I see the issue now—gmktemp simply replaces the trailing X's in the template string itself, preserving whatever directory path is already there. So I should just do a straightforward substitution of the X's and use the resulting string as the path, rather than trying to reconstruct it from separate components.\n\nWriting the file creation logic...\n\nActually, I'm realizing there's a bug with the error message—when I prepend the directory to the template, the error should still show the original template the user passed in, not the modified path with the directory added.\n\nBut thinking through this more carefully, the default template always has enough X's, so this edge case never actually triggers in practice. And for user-provided templates, I'm not prepending anything anyway since `useDefaultDir` is false. So the error message is fine as-i